# Allowlist, not a denylist.
#
# Everything is ignored by default and specific things are added back below.
# This is deliberate: a denylist fails open, and the one file that must never
# reach a remote (secrets.env, which holds the qBittorrent password and every
# *arr API key) would be committed the moment someone adds a new file type
# nobody thought to exclude. An allowlist fails closed instead.
#
# Before the first push, verify with:  git status --ignored
*

# --- structure ---------------------------------------------------------------
!.gitignore
!README.md
!LICENSE
!*/

# --- the actual stack definitions --------------------------------------------
!*/docker-compose.yml

# --- automation --------------------------------------------------------------
!*.sh
!*.ps1
!*.py
!glances-kiosk.bat

# Config files a compose file bind-mounts. server-room/glances.conf was ignored
# by the catch-all above while server-room/docker-compose.yml required it, so a
# clone-and-up rebuilt a BROKEN Glances: Docker Desktop auto-creates a directory
# at a bind-mount path whose source is missing, and `glances -C <directory>`
# does not fail loudly -- it silently reverts to the defaults the config exists
# to correct (empty sensors panel, three fake 1TB disks). The file also sits
# outside backup-volumes.sh's BIND_DIRS, so the live C: drive was its only copy.
# Any *.conf a stack mounts belongs in git for exactly this reason.
!*.conf

# The pre-commit secret guard has no extension, so the allowlist above would
# drop it. Enable it after cloning with: git config core.hooksPath .githooks
!.githooks/pre-commit

# --- templates (never the real thing) ----------------------------------------
!*.example

# --- re-ignore anything the broad rules above would let back in ---------------
# secrets.env holds live credentials. homarr/.env holds the key that decrypts
# every integration credential Homarr stores.
secrets.env
.env
*.env
!*.env.example

# Editor/agent scratch space, not infrastructure.
.claude/
.agents/
.vscode/

# Superseded copies of scripts. rolling-window.sh.bak-20260727 is a 30K
# snapshot of an older revision; git is the history mechanism now.
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.orig
*.prev-*

# Runtime state and generated output. These churn constantly and several of
# them (server-audit-report.txt, rolling-window.log) describe the media library
# in detail. watched-ledger.txt is irreplaceable operational state and is
# covered by the nightly backup, not by git.
*.log
*.txt
*.json
